Vegetable Soup Seasoning is a MSG-free seasoning blend that is also called vegetable soup mix, vegetable soup blend, or vegetable soup base.
Vegetable Soup Seasoning is a robust blend that adds bold flavor to hearty vegetable soups. It is popular with:
soup, sauce, meal mix, and frozen meal manufacturers;
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
seasoning companies;
catering companies;
cafes;
bakeries;
butchers; and
brewpubs
We also have a Vegetable Soup Base available.

Flavor Profile
Vegetable Soup Seasoning is savory, earthy, and peppery, with hints of lemony brightness.

How To Use
Use Vegetable Soup Seasoning to make a hearty Vegetable Soup. It is also terrific in a Vegetable Beef Soup. It can be used as a base for a vegetable lasagna or a vegetarian quiche. Mix it into the batter for savory Corn Fritters. Stir it into the filling for a savory Vegetable Tart. Use Vegetable Soup Seasoning as a seasoning for roasted or grilled vegetables, like Roasted Broccoli. It’s a great addition to Grilled Portobello Mushrooms. It can also be used as a rub for tofu or seitan.

Vermont Maple Sugar is a rich, sweet, and flavorful maple sugar that has been dehydrated. It is also called dehydrated maple sugar, maple sugar bulk, maple sugar powder, or maple sugar crystals. This sugar is made from the sap of the maple tree, and is often used as a substitute for granulated sugar in a wide variety of recipes.
Vermont Maple Sugar is popular with:
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
seasoning companies;
catering companies;
American restaurants;
breweries;
bakeries;
candy, popcorn, and sauce manufacturers; and
coffee shops
We also have Organic Vermont Maple Sugar available.

Flavor Profile
Vermont Maple Sugar has a sweet, rich flavor with notes of caramel and vanilla.

How To Use
Vermont Maple Sugar is a versatile sugar that can be used in a wide variety of recipes. It can be substituted for granulated sugar in all recipes. It is a great sugar to use in baking, and is excellent in cookies, cakes, scones, and muffins. It is delicious in frostings, fillings, and toppings. Add it to DIY pancake and waffle mixes, and blend into DIY seasoning blends. It is also delicious in savory applications, so try it in your favorite BBQ rub or in the glaze for your smoked ribs.

Vietnamese Pork Rub, a salt-free, MSG-free seasoning blend, is also known as Vietnamese pork seasoning, Vietnamese pork rub, or Vietnamese pork spice blend. It is a spicy, sweet, and aromatic blend that’s great for grilling, roasting, and smoking.
This versatile blend is popular with:
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
butchers;
barbecue, Asian, and American restaurants; and
manufacturers of sauces, condiments, and snack foods
We also have a traditional, salt-based Vietnamese Seasoning available.

Flavor Profile
Vietnamese Pork Rub is sweet, spicy, and earthy with notes of citrus and herbaceousness.

How To Use
Vietnamese Pork Rub is a great way to season pork. Use it to make Vietnamese Grilled Pork Skewers, or swap it in when making Vietnamese Spring Rolls. It’s also delicious with chicken, so swap it in when making the classic Vietnamese dish, Banh Mi. You can also use it to make Vietnamese Chicken Lettuce Wraps. Or, swap it in when making Grilled Pork Tenderloin. It’s a terrific rub for pork chops, pork roast, or even a pulled pork sandwich.

Whole Nutmeg, Myristica fragrans, is also called whole nutmeg, whole nutmegs, whole nutmeg spice, or whole nutmeg seeds. It is closely related to mace and is often found in the spice aisle with other products that are considered to be "exotic" spices. It is frequently purchased by:
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
seasoning companies;
catering companies;
breweries;
distilleries;
coffee shops;
bakeries; and
restaurants that specialize in Indian, Caribbean, or Asian cuisine
We also have Ground Nutmeg available.

Flavor Profile
Whole Nutmeg has a warm, sweet, and aromatic flavor with notes of pine and mint.

How To Use
Whole Nutmeg is the seed of an evergreen tree that grows in Indonesia. It is also known as the spice nutmeg. It is closely related to mace, which is the outer covering of the nutmeg seed. It is frequently purchased by:
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
seasoning companies;
catering companies;
breweries;
distilleries;
coffee shops;
bakeries; and
restaurants that specialize in Indian, Caribbean, or Asian cuisine
We also have Ground Nutmeg available.
Whole Nutmeg is most commonly used in baking. It is a must-have in pumpkin pie and spice cakes, and is a standard ingredient in gingerbread and other holiday treats. It is also used in savory dishes like curries and stews, or to add warmth to soups and chilis. It can be found in sausage, pickles, and some alcoholic beverages like eggnog and mulled wine. Nutmeg pairs well with cinnamon, allspice, ginger, chocolate, apples, bananas, eggplant, and cheeses.
Nutmeg is a versatile spice that can be used in a wide variety of dishes, from sweet to savory. It is used in baking, desserts, curries, stews, soups, chili, and pickles. It is also used in sausage, mulled wine, and eggnog. It can be found in pumpkin pie, gingerbread, spice cakes, and other holiday treats. It pairs well with cinnamon, allspice, ginger, chocolate, apples, bananas, eggplant, and cheeses.

Yellow Mustard Seeds, Brassica alba, are also called yellow mustard seed bulk, mustard seed bulk, bulk mustard seed, yellow mustard seed bulk, or yellow mustard seeds.
Yellow Mustard Seeds are the least pungent of the mustard seeds. They are often used as a base for milder mustards, like American-style mustard.
Businesses that frequently purchase Yellow Mustard Seeds are:
American and bar & grill restaurants;
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
bakeries;
catering companies;
butchers; and
breweries
We also have Brown Mustard Seeds and Organic Yellow Mustard Seeds available.

Flavor Profile
Yellow Mustard Seeds are spicy, peppery, and pungent.

How to Use
Yellow Mustard Seeds are a versatile spice that can be used in sweet and savory dishes. They are often used to make a mustard paste, which can be used as a base for a condiment. They are popular in Indian and African cooking and can be found in curries, soups, stews, and grilled meats. They can be used in pickling and are a common ingredient in Indian pickles, or achaar. Yellow Mustard Seeds are also used in breads, cakes, and biscuits.

Za'atar is a pungent, herbaceous, savory seasoning blend that is popular throughout the Middle East. It is also called zaatar, zaatar spice, zaatar seasoning, zaatar spice mix, zaatar spice blend, or zaatar seasoning blend.
Za'atar is a blend of spices that is popular in the Middle East. It is a seasoning blend that is as much a state of mind as it is a flavor. The word "za'atar" can refer to a variety of things. It can refer to the herb za'atar (hyssop), the leaves of which are used to make this blend. It can refer to the dry blend of herbs and spices that is mixed with olive oil and eaten as a condiment. It can also refer to the flat bread that is dipped in the condiment and eaten.
Za'atar is a versatile blend that can be used on just about anything. It is popular with:
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
Mediterranean restaurants;
bakeries;
breweries;
distilleries; and
American restaurants

Flavor Profile
Za'atar is earthy, herbaceous, and nutty with a little bit of citrus and a touch of sweetness.

How to Use
Za'atar is delicious with eggs, so sprinkle it on your scrambled eggs or make a frittata. It's terrific on roasted chicken, so toss over chicken thighs and roast. You can also stir it into yogurt or hummus. It's a wonderful topping for flatbreads, so use it on pita bread. Toss over roasted vegetables, like eggplant or zucchini, or stir into the marinade when making grilled eggplant. It's also delicious on grilled or roasted fish.

Za’atar Seasoning is a fragrant, earthy, and aromatic seasoning blend that is popular in Middle Eastern and African cuisines. It is also called zaatar spice, zaatar seasoning, zaatar spice blend, zaatar spice mix, or zaatar powder.
The word “za’atar” comes from the Arabic word za’atar, which means “wild thyme.” It is a generic name that describes a variety of herbs, spices, and blends, and it is often used to refer to the herb thyme. It is also a popular name for the spice blend that is commonly found in the Middle East and Africa.
Za’atar Seasoning is popular with:
Middle Eastern and Mediterranean restaurants;
seasoning companies;
independent spice shops;
olive oil shops;
brewpubs; and
companies that manufacture jams, sauces, and condiments

Flavor Profile
Za’atar Seasoning is earthy, nutty, and herbaceous with lemony top notes.

How To Use
Za’atar Seasoning is a versatile blend that is a natural on a variety of dishes. It is a classic blend to use when making a traditional Za’atar Manakeesh, a flatbread that is topped with Za’atar Seasoning and baked. It is also delicious on breads like pita and naan, so try it as a topping on Pita Pizza. It’s a delicious addition to Hummus, and can be used to season grilled or roasted vegetables like eggplant, carrots, or zucchini. It can be used to season chicken, fish, or lamb. Sprinkle it over roasted chickpeas or add to the marinade when making Grilled Chicken Kebabs. It’s delicious in the seasoning mix when making Sausage and Egg Breakfast Burritos. It’s a great blend to use when making a traditional Middle Eastern dish like Mujadara, a simple stew of lentils and rice.
